 ## Operating characteristics
-**Time Complexity:** $O(1)$ per pixel for the shading calculation itself, assuming the surface position and normal are already known from the ray marching step. The overall complexity is dominated by the ray marching step, which is $O(N)$ where $N$ is the number of steps to reach the surface [G].
-**Memory Complexity:** $O(1)$ additional memory per pixel for lighting calculations [G].
-**Strengths:** Computationally inexpensive; provides physically plausible diffuse shading for implicit surfaces; easy to implement [G].
-**Limitations:** Does not account for inter-reflections (global illumination); specular highlights are absent; sensitive to normal estimation errors from finite differences [G].
-**When to Use:** When real-time performance is critical and only diffuse lighting is required; for simple implicit object rendering [G].
-**When Not to Use:** When realistic global illumination or specular effects are needed; when high-precision normals are critical and finite difference noise is unacceptable [G].
+*   **Time Complexity:** $O(1)$ per surface point evaluation, assuming the normal is already computed. The cost is dominated by the dot product and vector normalization [S2, unofficial][S4, unofficial].
+*   **Memory Complexity:** $O(1)$, requiring only temporary storage for vectors and scalars [S2, unofficial].
+*   **Strengths:** Computationally inexpensive; provides physically plausible diffuse shading for implicit surfaces; easy to implement [S3, unofficial][S4, unofficial].
+*   **Limitations:** Does not account for inter-reflections (global illumination); flat appearance without ambient or SSAO; sensitive to normal estimation errors in SDFs [S5].
+*   **When to Use:** Real-time rendering of SDF objects where performance is critical; base layer for more complex shading models [S1, unofficial][S4, unofficial].
+*   **When Not to Use:** When specular highlights are required (use [Phong Shading with SDF Normals](1977-phong-shading-with-sdf-normals.md)); when high-fidelity global illumination is needed [S5].
 
 ## Background
-Lambertian reflectance is a fundamental model in computer graphics, describing a perfectly diffuse surface that scatters light equally in all directions [G]. Its application to SDFs leverages the ability of SDFs to provide continuous surface representations, allowing for shading without explicit mesh data [G].
+Lambertian lighting is a fundamental model in computer graphics, originally derived from Lambert's Cosine Law [S3, unofficial]. In the context of SDFs, it has been widely adopted in ray marching tutorials and implementations to provide basic shading for implicit surfaces [S1, unofficial][S3, unofficial][S4, unofficial]. It is commonly used in educational resources and real-time visualization tools to enhance the perception of shape and depth [S5].
 
